<p>Temple is where we live and work, so our crews don't have a long drive to most of the city. That matters after a spring hailstorm, when dozens of homeowners are calling at once and a short trip means we can see more roofs in a day. It also means we know how differently roofs age from one part of town to another. This page covers what Temple homeowners ask us most and how a local inspection works. You'll also find how we schedule work and how the housing mix around the city shapes the roofing decisions people make.</p>

<h2>One City, Several Very Different Roofs</h2>
<p>Temple grew as a railroad town and later as a medical and distribution hub, and its housing shows each stage. A Temple roofer can work on a 1920s home in the morning and a five-year-old subdivision house that afternoon. The problems on those two roofs usually have little in common.</p>

<h3>Older homes in central Temple</h3>
<p>Many homes near downtown and in the older central neighborhoods have steeper roofs, multiple layers of past repairs, and wood decking that has been through decades of heat. Some have had a second layer of shingles installed over the first, which hides the condition of the deck and adds weight. Chimneys, dormers and porch tie-ins are common leak points on these houses. When we work on an older home we look closely at the decking and flashing, because a new shingle over a soft or poorly flashed area will not last.</p>

<h3>Mid-century brick ranch homes</h3>
<p>Temple has a lot of single-story brick homes with low-to-moderate pitch roofs, often on their second or third shingle roof by now. Their weak spots are usually attic ventilation that was never upgraded, aging pipe boots and worn valleys. These roofs are often good candidates for honest repair-versus-replace conversations, which we cover in <a href="/blog/roof-repair-vs-replacement-temple/">Roof Repair vs. Roof Replacement</a>.</p>

<h3>Newer subdivisions in west and south Temple</h3>
<p>The growth on the west and south sides of Temple brought plenty of newer homes with complex roof lines: several gables, hips, valleys and roof-to-wall transitions. Even a fairly young roof can take hail or wind damage, and builder-grade shingles and step flashing sometimes show problems earlier than owners expect. If you are in an HOA, check with your HOA about any color or material requirements before choosing a replacement shingle.</p>

<h3>Commercial and multi-family buildings</h3>
<p>Along the I-35 corridor and around Temple's commercial areas you will find low-slope and flat roofs on retail, office, warehouse and multi-family buildings. Those systems need different materials and drainage planning than a house. Our <a href="/services/commercial-roofing-temple-tx/">commercial roofing</a> service covers inspections, leak repair and replacement planning for these buildings.</p>

<h2>What Temple Homeowners Ask Us Most</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>"Was that last storm bad enough to damage my roof?"</strong> Sometimes yes, sometimes no. Hail can bruise shingles without obvious signs from the ground, so a close inspection is the only reliable answer. See <a href="/blog/how-to-spot-hail-damage-on-your-roof-temple-tx/">How to Spot Hail Damage on Your Roof</a> for what to look for safely.</li>
<li><strong>"Can this be repaired, or do I need a new roof?"</strong> If a repair will do the job, we will tell you. Replacement makes sense when damage is widespread, the roof is near the end of its life, or repeated repairs are adding up.</li>
<li><strong>"Should I file an insurance claim?"</strong> That is your decision. We can document damage with photos and write a repair estimate, but we are roofers, not public adjusters, and we do not negotiate claims for you.</li>
<li><strong>"Why is my upstairs so hot?"</strong> Summer heat plus poor attic ventilation is a common cause, and it also shortens shingle life.</li>
</ul>

